00:29:00.540 --> 00:29:01.930
So my name is Yuri.

00:29:01.930 --> 00:29:04.260
I'm the tech lead
for VPC Flow Logs.

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:07.500
And I kind of wanted to give
you some engineering background

00:29:07.500 --> 00:29:11.670
as to how we generate VPC Flow
Logs and what are the methods

00:29:11.670 --> 00:29:13.140
that we are using.

00:29:13.140 --> 00:29:15.390
And that's kind of to
give you a general feeling

00:29:15.390 --> 00:29:19.470
as to the capabilities
of these two.

00:29:19.470 --> 00:29:25.650
So to generate VPC Flow Logs, we
start by sampling connections.

00:29:25.650 --> 00:29:29.100
We sample packet headers
and all connections

00:29:29.100 --> 00:29:32.160
coming through and from a VM.

00:29:32.160 --> 00:29:34.530
And by sampling
packet headers we

00:29:34.530 --> 00:29:38.160
are able to monitor
connection behavior over time.

00:29:38.160 --> 00:29:39.690
If you have on-prem
setups, you're

00:29:39.690 --> 00:29:43.200
probably familiar with tools
such as NetFlow and sFlow,

00:29:43.200 --> 00:29:46.650
which have a very
similar approach.

00:29:46.650 --> 00:29:49.920
So from every packet
that we sample,

00:29:49.920 --> 00:29:53.010
we extract IPs,
ports, and protocol

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:56.300
in order to uniquely
identify a connection.

00:29:56.300 --> 00:29:59.580
We sample as close
to the VM as possible

00:29:59.580 --> 00:30:02.780
in order to ensure that we are
seeing the same IPs addresses

00:30:02.780 --> 00:30:04.920
as specified by the VM.

00:30:04.920 --> 00:30:06.750
And this ensures
us that we will be

00:30:06.750 --> 00:30:10.530
able to correlate VPC Flow
Logs with other data sources

00:30:10.530 --> 00:30:11.880
that you might have--

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:13.710
for example, if
you're running agents

00:30:13.710 --> 00:30:18.210
on your VM to collect
the same type of data.

00:30:18.210 --> 00:30:21.060
Every five seconds we
combine all of the headers

00:30:21.060 --> 00:30:24.540
that we've seen for
each connection.

00:30:24.540 --> 00:30:27.175
And by keeping track
of sequence numbers

00:30:27.175 --> 00:30:30.540
we are able to estimate packet
count on the connection.

00:30:30.540 --> 00:30:35.670
By looking at the payload size
we are calculating throughput.

00:30:35.670 --> 00:30:40.410
And for TCP connections where
enough packets were captured,

00:30:40.410 --> 00:30:42.810
we are able to
correlate [INAUDIBLE]

00:30:42.810 --> 00:30:47.467
time stamps to estimate RTT as
experienced by the connection.

00:30:50.210 --> 00:30:53.180
So gathering information
is one thing.

00:30:53.180 --> 00:30:58.140
But making sure that it's
accurate is kind of difficult.

00:30:58.140 --> 00:31:02.260
So for network monitoring
tools, accuracy

00:31:02.260 --> 00:31:05.090
is of the highest importance.

00:31:05.090 --> 00:31:08.330
To make sure that we
have high accuracy,

00:31:08.330 --> 00:31:13.010
we try to set our sampling rate
to the highest value possible.

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:17.420
To do that we do not just
set a constant sampling rate.

00:31:17.420 --> 00:31:20.900
But we adjust our
sampling rate in real time

00:31:20.900 --> 00:31:23.690
to the highest value
possible without exceeding

00:31:23.690 --> 00:31:26.640
set resources.

00:31:26.640 --> 00:31:30.360
This sampling rate is
being captured per packet

00:31:30.360 --> 00:31:33.120
to make sure that we are
compensating correctly

00:31:33.120 --> 00:31:36.828
for the packets
that we do not see.

00:31:36.828 --> 00:31:41.900
Furthermore, we then
measure the accuracy

00:31:41.900 --> 00:31:45.370
of our system against a
separate [INAUDIBLE] system

00:31:45.370 --> 00:31:48.610
just to make sure that our
pipeline is not only available

00:31:48.610 --> 00:31:50.165
but that its accuracy is intact.

00:31:54.030 --> 00:31:58.770
Although IPs and ports are
the backbone of our reports,

00:31:58.770 --> 00:32:04.050
it's not necessarily the
most useful identifiers

00:32:04.050 --> 00:32:06.510
for VPC Flow Logs.

00:32:06.510 --> 00:32:10.120
Sometimes when you look
at connections of VMs,

00:32:10.120 --> 00:32:12.480
for example, that
no longer exist

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:14.780
or you look at the
IP addresses that

00:32:14.780 --> 00:32:17.280
do not belong to
your network, those

00:32:17.280 --> 00:32:19.880
are hard to make sense of.

00:32:19.880 --> 00:32:23.960
In order to tackle that
problem while we process

00:32:23.960 --> 00:32:27.140
the data in real time,
we combine the data

00:32:27.140 --> 00:32:30.980
with metadata from your project.

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:38.390
And we are able to annotate GCP
endpoints with VM identifiers,

00:32:38.390 --> 00:32:40.280
VPC identifiers.

00:32:40.280 --> 00:32:42.710
And for external
endpoints, we are

00:32:42.710 --> 00:32:46.760
able to annotate geolocation
such as country, city,

00:32:46.760 --> 00:32:51.650
and region, which enables
you to search and filter

00:32:51.650 --> 00:32:54.680
VPC Flow Logs by those fields.

00:32:54.680 --> 00:32:56.780
So for example, if you're
interested in seeing

00:32:56.780 --> 00:32:59.880
traffic between two
VMs, you can do that.

00:32:59.880 --> 00:33:01.880
Or if you're interested
in seeing traffic

00:33:01.880 --> 00:33:04.530
from a certain region
to a certain country,

00:33:04.530 --> 00:33:05.530
you can do that as well.

00:33:08.970 --> 00:33:12.230
So there are a lot of use
cases for network monitoring

00:33:12.230 --> 00:33:17.450
tools, many of which
require data that is fresh.

00:33:17.450 --> 00:33:19.110
One of those use cases--

00:33:19.110 --> 00:33:20.780
one that is close to my heart--

00:33:20.780 --> 00:33:24.440
is debugging
large-scale systems.

00:33:24.440 --> 00:33:29.160
And our collection system
was built with that in mind.

00:33:29.160 --> 00:33:33.170
So after collecting
data for five seconds,

00:33:33.170 --> 00:33:37.100
we spend additional four
second processing this data.

00:33:37.100 --> 00:33:40.280
And we assure those quick
times by making sure

00:33:40.280 --> 00:33:43.970
that our pipeline is
distributed in all cloud zones

00:33:43.970 --> 00:33:47.090
so that the data
is being processed

00:33:47.090 --> 00:33:49.670
near where it was sampled.

00:33:49.670 --> 00:33:51.770
Then after additional
10 seconds,

00:33:51.770 --> 00:33:55.560
this data is available
for your use.

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:58.790
So if you're running a
scenario on your network,

00:33:58.790 --> 00:34:04.570
you're able to see relevant
VPC Flow Logs for this scenario

00:34:04.570 --> 00:34:07.760
in under 20 seconds
from when it happened.

00:34:10.480 --> 00:34:14.409
Now every GCP project is unique.

00:34:14.409 --> 00:34:17.860
Some projects are small
and have very few VMs,

00:34:17.860 --> 00:34:22.510
but some projects are large and
have very complicated networks.

00:34:22.510 --> 00:34:24.610
Our pipeline
currently is already

00:34:24.610 --> 00:34:28.690
handling a very large
portion of GCP traffic--

00:34:28.690 --> 00:34:32.130
those belonging to early
adopters of VCP Flow

00:34:32.130 --> 00:34:33.645
Logs and beta users.

00:34:37.560 --> 00:34:40.940
So the amount of VPC
Flow Logs currently

00:34:40.940 --> 00:34:44.870
amounts to millions
of records per second.

00:34:44.870 --> 00:34:50.150
And we experience no performance
impact on user compute

00:34:50.150 --> 00:34:52.989
or network resources.

00:34:52.989 --> 00:34:54.770
So you should not
hesitate enabling

00:34:54.770 --> 00:34:57.910
VCP Flow Logs for your network.

00:34:57.910 --> 00:35:01.050
Keep in mind that
enabling those logs

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:04.230
may result in substantial
amount of traffic.

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:08.340
But BigQuery, cloud
logging, and Pub/Sub

00:35:08.340 --> 00:35:12.420
are provisioned to handle them
if you choose to use them.

00:35:12.420 --> 00:35:14.950
So as long as you
can ingest this data,

00:35:14.950 --> 00:35:16.100
we are able to provide it.

00:35:18.620 --> 00:35:23.730
The other project our team is
working on is firewall logging.

00:35:23.730 --> 00:35:28.260
So firewall logging is collected
differently than VPC Flow Logs,

00:35:28.260 --> 00:35:30.600
because the use cases
for firewall logging

00:35:30.600 --> 00:35:34.110
are a bit different than
those of VPC Flow Logs.

00:35:34.110 --> 00:35:37.200
For VPC Flow Logs,
we wanted to ensure

00:35:37.200 --> 00:35:39.420
that our users are
able to monitor

00:35:39.420 --> 00:35:42.330
connection behavior over time.

00:35:42.330 --> 00:35:45.540
For firewall logging, the
existence of the connection

00:35:45.540 --> 00:35:49.960
is more important
than its behavior.

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:54.240
So firewall logs are
generated per firewall event.

00:35:54.240 --> 00:35:57.240
So every time the
connection is created

00:35:57.240 --> 00:36:01.320
or a connection is denied, a
relevant plug will be created.

00:36:01.320 --> 00:36:06.150
To do so, we keep a
connection table for every VM.

00:36:06.150 --> 00:36:09.480
And every time we add the
new connection to the table,

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:12.810
we will generate
a firewall rule--

00:36:12.810 --> 00:36:14.760
firewall log.

00:36:14.760 --> 00:36:17.270
For connections
that were denied,

00:36:17.270 --> 00:36:20.880
we will report each
time that a connection

00:36:20.880 --> 00:36:27.100
was attempted in order to
enable security use cases.

00:36:27.100 --> 00:36:31.886
By taking this approach,
we are able to guarantee

00:36:31.886 --> 00:36:35.380
with higher confidence
that we will report

00:36:35.380 --> 00:36:37.660
on every relevant connection.

00:36:40.540 --> 00:36:44.440
So firewall logs-- very
similar to VPC Flow Logs--

00:36:44.440 --> 00:36:47.890
are reported in near
real time, which

00:36:47.890 --> 00:36:51.330
means that after a
connection is created

00:36:51.330 --> 00:36:54.885
you are able to see a relevant
log in just a few seconds.

00:36:57.550 --> 00:37:01.060
We guarantee that
we can capture up

00:37:01.060 --> 00:37:04.450
to 100 connections per
second for every core that

00:37:04.450 --> 00:37:07.330
is provisioned for your VM.

00:37:07.330 --> 00:37:11.260
And we do that without
any performance impact

00:37:11.260 --> 00:37:14.930
on your compute resources.

00:37:14.930 --> 00:37:18.110
Firewall logs, just
as VPC Flow Logs,

00:37:18.110 --> 00:37:23.270
are annotated in real time with
metadata from your project.

00:37:23.270 --> 00:37:28.590
And in addition to annotating
VM instances and geolocations,

00:37:28.590 --> 00:37:33.080
we also combine those logs
with relevant firewall rules

00:37:33.080 --> 00:37:37.480
so that you can easily use them.

00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:38.400
Thank you.

